<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindola Mahal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hindola Mahal ('swinging palace') is an audience hall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built by the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in the 15th century. Its massive sloping side walls give the impression that the building is swaying. It stands within the royal enclosure near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahaz-mahal">Jahaz Mahal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hindola-mahal.jpg" alt="Hindola Mahal" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muk.khan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindola_Mahal,_Mandu,_India.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hindola Mahal might have been built in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah">Hoshang Shah</a>, about 1425, but it may date from the end of the 15th century, in the reign of Ghiyas-al-Din. It is one of a set of buildings that make up the royal palace complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahaz-mahal">Jahaz Mahal</a>, the Tawili Mahal and the Nahar Jharokha, and it may have served as an audience chamber. Its plan is a T-shape, though the exterior walls suggest that the crossbar was added later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The stem of the T is a single hall 30 by 18 metres and 12 metres high. Its side walls are 3 metres thick and reinforced by massive buttresses inclined at over 77 degrees, which give the building its name and take the outward thrust of the five great ogee arches that once carried its flat roof. Each long side has six recessed arches holding doors below and three slabs of open-work stone windows above, which let in air and light, and a high wall carries battlements on the roof.</p><p>The crossbar is of about the same proportions as the hall but rises in two tiers. Its upper floor, divided into two rooms, looks into the main hall through an arched opening, and the ground floor holds a cruciform gallery leading to another entrance and to an arch into the hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The building shows the marks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> style of 1400 to 1550, which favoured simplicity, boldness and good proportion: it has little ornament and stands out through its massive sloping buttresses, an exaggerated example of the style, while the pointed arches inside show the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. An almost exact copy, possibly by the same architect, stands in the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>; it is smaller and, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a> hall, holds a water cistern in the middle of its main hall. The Hindola Mahal is now a tourist destination in the ruined city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hindola Mahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mandu's Jahaz Mahal, <b>Hindola Mahal</b>, Jama Masjid and Hoshang Shah's marble tomb show a distinctive Afghan style.</blockquote><a href="/state/malwa-sultanate">Malwa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
